From: Chad Monroe <chad@monroe.io>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 16:30:00 -0700
Subject: net: airoha: ppe: make full-table FOE scans preemptible

Every clean_entry_by_* request runs on clean_entry_wq (WQ_UNBOUND,
max_active 1) as a linear walk of airoha_ppe_get_total_num_entries()
entries, 32768 on the AN7581, calling airoha_ppe_foe_get_entry() per
entry; the 16384 SRAM entries each cost a REG_PPE_RAM_CTRL request, an
ACK poll and 20 register reads. Measured 55 to 61 ms per work item with
no scheduling point inside. arht_wifi_lo expires a session after 1 s of
transmit idle and queues a CLEAN_BY_PORT scan for each, so an iperf3 run
queued 316 items in 15 s, and on this PREEMPT_NONE kernel a SCHED_FIFO
voice engine thread on the same CPU waited the whole item every time
(wake-to-run 54 ms, then a playback underrun and an xrun).

Add arht_ppe_scan_relax(): cond_resched() every 128 entries, about
0.45 ms at 3.5 us per SRAM entry, called with ppe_lock released, at the
top of the loop body of every process-context scanner: by_gemport,
by_channel, by_fport_and_channel, by_mac_common, by_type (which covers
clean_multicast_entry), by_ip, by_port and by_landev. by_landev is also
reached from the netdev notifier under RTNL, which is process context.
reuse_shrink_table() runs under shnk_tbl_lock and
airoha_ppe_clean_sram_table() holds ppe_lock across its loop; both stay
as they are, the latter reached only by the blapi clean-table command.

The scan still costs the same CPU time; it no longer holds the CPU. A
single-entry delete at session expiry, by the FOE index wifi_lo already
records for the ingress direction plus the egress index it does not,
would remove the scan; it is not safe alone because PPE ageing is never
enabled and an undeleted entry leaks its slot.

Signed-off-by: Chad Monroe <chad@monroe.io>
---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/arht_ppe.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/arht_ppe.c
@@ -14,6 +14,7 @@
 #include <linux/netdevice.h>
 #include <linux/vmalloc.h>
 #include <linux/workqueue.h>
+#include <linux/sched.h>
 #include <linux/notifier.h>
 
 #include "airoha_eth.h"
@@ -82,6 +83,26 @@ static struct workqueue_struct *clean_en
 *************************************************************************
 */
 extern spinlock_t ppe_lock;
+
+/*
+ * A full-table FOE scan walks airoha_ppe_get_total_num_entries() entries
+ * (32768 on the AN7581) at 55 to 61 ms per scan, and the 16384 SRAM entries
+ * carry almost all of it: each is a REG_PPE_RAM_CTRL request, an ACK poll
+ * and 20 register reads, about 3.5 us. Nothing else runs on that CPU until
+ * the scanner blocks or returns, so the scanners give the scheduler a turn
+ * every 128 entries, about 0.45 ms, which keeps the wake of a SCHED_FIFO
+ * task on the same CPU under a millisecond. Call only with ppe_lock
+ * released: spin_lock_bh() raises the preempt count even without kernel
+ * preemption.
+ */
+#define ARHT_PPE_SCAN_RELAX_INTERVAL	128
+
+static inline void arht_ppe_scan_relax(unsigned int idx)
+{
+	if ((idx & (ARHT_PPE_SCAN_RELAX_INTERVAL - 1)) == 0)
+		cond_resched();
+}
+
 extern int (*ra_sw_nat_hook_clean_table) (void);
 
 int (*ra_sw_nat_hook_drop_packet) (struct sk_buff * skb) = NULL;
